I had intended to try ascending an Elf ever since first ascending in
3.2, but sometime since then, the Elf class has been replaced by the
more general Ranger... but the way I figure it, an Elven Ranger is
pretty close to what an Elf used to be, so that's what I picked.

Took me a few tries to get up and running; one thing I had to figure
out is that a bow being the alternate weapon is not enough to get the
super-chucking power that it conveys.  After determining this, I found
the bow to be a pretty effective weapon, even more so advancing to
Expert in bows and often firing as many as four arrows per move.

Unfortunately, I would usually run out of arrows before finding any
decent sources of replenishment. Dismantling arrow traps would get me
a few dozen, but they weren't much of a replacement for the +2
enchanted arrows I started with. Fortunately, I found some gauntlets
of power, which reduced the need to use ranged weapons.

The Ranger quest starts in a forest -- that's funny! It uses the same
map generation code as the Gnomish mines, except using trees rather
than stone walls. It actually works pretty well! I didn't actually
count the rooms or their exits in the wumpus level, but it seems
plausible that they made it true to the Hunt the Wumpus dodecahedron
layout. Scorpius was a pushover, and the ability of the Longbow of
Diana to create arrows is pretty sweet. By the end of the game, I had
over 300 blessed +7 arrows. Not that I used them much, sadly.

In a failed previous attempt, I also relearned that shopkeepers are
jerks. Fire arrows in their shop to clear out their mimic infestation,
and they claim ownership of the arrows. As a result, I suddenly lost
any qualms I had previously had about killing shopkeepers -- and of
course, elves are chaotic in Nethack, so it was perfectly in character
as well. Every shopkeeper in the game died, except for Izchak, whom I
spared out of pity. Seriously now, how would you like to be
reincarnated in a computer game as a jerk?

Soon after that, I realized that I had a pack full of unidentified
stuff and no scrolls of ID left; this might be the game where I really
would turn out to need price identification. Luckily, I had found a
magic marker in a grave, earlier in the dungeon. I wrote a few blessed
scrolls of identify and got the full-pack ID after a few tries. At
that point, the main notable items I had were gauntlets of power and a
bag of holding.

Encountering a barracks without any source of reflection caused some
consternation. I dawdled a bit, but couldn't find any. Eventually I
just decided to take them on. One of them had a wand of death ... the
ray missed me, bounced off a wall, missed me again, and killed the
zapper. Phew. A wand of lightning destroyed the wand of death, a ring
of free action and a ring of conflict. Unfortunate, but not too bad.

Getting to Medusa without levitation or reflection was a first for me,
IIRC, and I eventually dug my way into the mazes below. Unfortunately,
the Medusa statues didn't have any source of levitation or reflection.
Well, no way to get back now, time to go on to the castle.

Whoops, I don't have a musical instrument either... no way to get to
the back entrance and no way to get in the front entrance either. Was
I going to have to go into Gehennom without clearing the castle and
getting my well-manicured hands on the wand of wishing? I decided now
was the time to use up my magic lamp... if I don't get a wish, then
I'll dig through the floor. But I did, and I got the ring of
levitation that I asked for. I was a bit apprehensive about the fight,
especially because there were a few rust monsters, disenchanters and
liches in there, as I had yet to find any scrolls of genocide either.

It worked out alright. I ended up going melee when nothing dangerous
was nearby and falling back to use the longbow when an R or L came
close. I used the wand to get some much needed speed boots and shield
of reflection, and a gray dragon scale mail, as I hadn't found any
decent source magic resistance yet either. Feeling much safer, I
ventured into Gehennom.

I encountered a mind flayer in Asmodeus's lair. Nasty! He sucked at my
brains at least once before I killed him, resulting in severe memory
loss -- most of the levels and a lot of the items. Oh well... I still
remembered all the really scary scrolls, and I had been digging paths
through Gehennom anyways -- just follow the carved holes.

Not a lot else happened on the way down. I found the vibrating square,
marked it with a gold piece. It took me a while to find Vlad's tower
entrance on the way back up, but once I took out Vlad, the next step
was was to take on the Wizard, and I headed back to the castle to beef
up.

Alchemized a bunch of potions into blessed extra healing, for the 5hp
bonus per each. Polymorphed scrolls, one ended up being genocide. Or
maybe I had found that earlier, I don't rightly recall... ended up
with several enchant armor and enchant weapon. Used the magic marker
to write some more genocide and enchant armor scrolls, then wished for
another magic marker and emptied that one on enchantment scrolls too.

Ended up vaporizing my speed boots because I had assumed I was
confused by a difficult spell -- nope, just stunned. Luckily, I had
found a spare set in Gehennom and stashed it away in my bag of
holding. No real loss, but I was lucky that I had that spare!

Killed the Wizard. Invocation time:
You turn the pages of the Book of the Dead...
The floor shakes violently under you!
The walls around you begin to bend and crumble!
You are standing at the top of a stairwell leading down!
The elf-lord drowns.

Whoops. Sorry about that.

The Wizard resurrected himself seven times, starting even before I had
the amulet yet. I had to go melee with him a couple times before I
remembered the scroll of charging I had and used it on Orcus's wand of
death.

The planes went quickly, and my HP never dropped very low. Conflict
helped immensely in the Astral Plane; the hostile angels that spawned
pretty much ignored me and wailed on each other as I sped off towards
Famine, who took a death ray to the head. The altar he was guarding
was mine, that was that, and I didn't even have to use my helm of
opposite alignment.

Speaking of that helm, I decided to always try to have one, or access
to one, after a Wizard died to Pestilence in a rather soul-destroying
fashion.  I'm sure this nasty cycle has happened to a lot of you: get
sick, cure self, get sick, cure self, get sick, cure self, get a blow
in, get sick, die, put on another amulet of life saving.  The unicorn
horn just isn't up to snuff to cure the illness he gives you, and I
burned through maybe three amulets of life-saving before finally
succumbing.

After I lost, of course, I realized several mistakes I had made, the
first of which was not using up my magic lamp for a helm of opposite
alignment when I reached the first altar.  Pissing off the gods by
attacking a coaligned priest with stormbringer hadn't helped either,
then there was not taking the amulet's drain on my energy into
consideration when I was casting teleport away at everything and not
having enough PW left to magic missile Pestilence.

On a related note, I saw almost the exact same situation happen to
someone else on alt.org's voyeur service; I probably shouldn't name
names, but judging by our respective ascension counts on the server,
this person had a lot more experience than I do.  I was hoping to see
some sort of coup, but the main difference between what I saw happen
and what happened to me was that I only burned through three amulets
of life-saving rather than seven.  I really felt sick to my stomach
watching those events unfold in front of me, far worse than I did when
it was happening to me.

